\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Judges Have Ordered Federal Workers Back to Their Jobs. Now What?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eileen Sullivan and Zach Montague \par \uc2 1,304 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 March 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Fired employees expressed excitement about the prospect of reinstatement, as well as back pay. But there's no template for rehiring en masse.\par \par \uc2 Last month, thousands of employees with probationary status across the federal government were fired by the Trump administration in an extraordinary and coordinated move. On Thursday, a pair of court rulings called for agencies to reinstate an untold number of them.\par \par \uc2 What happens now isn't so clear cut.\par \par \uc2 Agencies are sorting out how to bring back these employees and give them the back pay ordered by the courts. Some of the fired workers may indeed return to their jobs. Others may be placed on administrative leave until their agencies undergo a round of large-scale layoffs, the planning for which is already underway.\par \par \uc2 The mass firings of probationary workers were just one early phase of President Trump's aggressive plan to shrink the federal government. His administration appeared to target probationary employees because they do not have the same civil service protections as employees who have been in their job longer. But a flurry of challenges to the legality of how Trump officials went about ordering up the personnel changes have resulted in some reprieves, at least temporarily or on paper.\par \par \uc2 In interviews and on social media, fired employees expressed excitement about being reinstated and getting paid for the days since they were fired. Still, many employees are in the dark, learning details about their livelihood through media reports.\par \par \uc2 Here is what we know about the reinstatements, and what we don't.\par \par \uc2 What did the judges order?\par \par \uc2 The rulings, in federal courts in California and Maryland, call for a pause in the firings and reinstatement of probationary employees across 19 agencies. The cases themselves will continue to move forward, with the government planning to appeal.\par \par \uc2 But the plaintiffs' goals were to at least temporarily stop the administration from firing more probationary workers and obtain relief, such as back pay, for the employees already out of work.\par \par \uc2 The judges ruled that the firings were carried out unlawfully in accordance with orders from the Office of Personnel Management, the government's human resources office. Only the agencies themselves have the authority to direct those personnel changes, one of the judges wrote.\par \par \uc2 Judge James Bredar of the U.S. District Court for the District of Maryland restricted the government from firing any more probationary workers for two weeks. Judge Bredar said the employees covered in the lawsuit, who are from 18 different agencies, must be reinstated by March 17.\par \par \uc2 Judge William H. Alsup of the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of California ordered the government to pause firings and reinstate probationary employees at six agencies while the case continues. His order applied to the Pentagon, the Treasury, and the departments of Agriculture, Energy, Veterans Affairs, and the Interior.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers representing those groups estimated at least 10,000 people were affected across those agencies, numbers more or less consistent with data collected by The Times.\par \par \uc2 The judge's orders follow a similar decision handed down by the \uc2 Merit Systems Protection Board\uc2 , an independent administrative body that reviews government personnel decisions. It ordered on March 5 that certain probationary employees, mostly from the Department of Agriculture, be reinstated for at least 45 days.\par \par \uc2 What have the fired employees heard?\par \par \uc2 It depends on who you ask. There does not appear to be a uniform way that agencies are going about reinstating fired probationary employees.\par \par \uc2 Tim Kauffman, a spokesman for the American Federation of Government Workers, which is involved in one of the cases, said the union does not know how many of its members will be offered their jobs back. Mr. Kauffman said agencies had denied union requests for the number of fired probationary employees.\par \par \uc2 The union representing workers for the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  sent an email to probationary employees who were fired, informing them that they were in the process of speaking with agency management about the next steps. In the email, shared with \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , the \uc2 National Treasury Employees Union\uc2  said employees with one agency -- the Energy Department -- have started receiving reinstatement notifications after the court orders on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 ''We are pressing other agencies to issue reinstatement notices as quickly as possible,'' the email stated. The Energy Department did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Some employees from the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  were notified of their reinstatement through an email Thursday from the agency's human resources division.\par \par \uc2 ''Upon further review, the agency has determined to rescind the letter sent to you on 2/15/2025,'' the email stated, adding that the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  will work with them on a return to their jobs. The agency did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Some fired probationary employees from the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2  have heard from the agency's human resources division that reinstatements are underway, according to Cat Farman, the president of the local chapter of the employee union. The agency did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Other fired employees, however, are still getting ''off-boarding'' messages from the agency, Ms. Farman said, such as reminders to turn in their government-issued equipment.\par \par \uc2 Does this mean employees will immediately be back at work?\par \par \uc2 Not necessarily.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Agriculture, for example, said in a statement this week that it had returned all its fired probationary workers to ''pay status'' as of Wednesday. The statement did not say how many, or if any, workers would be returning to their jobs.\par \par \uc2 ''The department will work quickly to develop a phased plan for return to duty, and while those plans materialize, all probationary employees will be paid,'' the statement said.\par \par \uc2 But it was not clear that similar information was communicated to all of the fired employees at the agency. The agency did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm getting really frustrated,'' said Jacob Bushno, one of the probationary employees fired. He said he has not received any communications from the agency, and that he had reached out to his human resources department and his managers.\par \par \uc2 ''Zero. No guidance,'' he said on Friday. Mr. Bushno, a veteran who did two tours in Iraq while he was in the Army's air assault division, was fired just seven days before he completed his one-year probationary period at the Forest Service.\par \par \uc2 ''When will we get paid/back pay? Do we get to come back to the office?'' he asked.\par \par \uc2 A probationary employee who was fired from \uc2 Housing and Urban Development\uc2  last month similarly has not heard from the agency. The employee spoke on condition of anonymity out of fear of retribution. The housing agency did not respond to a request for comment. Ashaki Robinson, a representative for the union that represents workers at the agency, said the union has not heard of any fired employees hearing from the agency as of late Friday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Can the Trump administration still move forward with other layoffs?\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 The judge's rulings do not protect anyone from mass firings through other methods in the future. As the rulings came down on Thursday, federal agencies were finalizing plans to cut an even larger swath of the federal work force.\par \par \uc2 In the Maryland case, the judge told the government that it couldn't carry out future mass firings without prior notice as required by law.\par \par \uc2 In the case in California, the judge made plain that agencies planning to conduct large-scale layoffs, known as a ''reduction in force,'' can still proceed in accordance with the laws that govern such processes -- meaning that the reprieves for workers may only be temporary.\par \par \uc2 Apoorva Mandavilli contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Apoorva Mandavilli contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250317el3h00034\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
