\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 What-You-Did-Last-Week Inquiry Wins Musk Few Friends in Italy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Bubola \par \uc2 1,071 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 March 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employees at the Aviano Air Base who serve American forces got a familiar demand to list their achievements. Unions say Italy ''is not the Wild West like the U.S.''\par \par \uc2 Italian employees at the Aviano Air Base in northern Italy paused from flipping burgers, unloading trucks and restocking shelves recently to open an email from their bosses demanding that they list five key accomplishments from last week.\par \par \uc2 The email was a by-now familiar demand from President Trump's chief cost-cutter, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , carrying with it the threat of termination if they did not respond. But on this occasion, it did not land with government employees in the United States, but rather in Italy, a country where workers' rights are held sacrosanct.\par \par \uc2 The result set the stage for a puzzling clash of cultures, with the world's richest man and his job-thrashing chain saw on one side, and one of the world's most protective champions of the forever job on the other.\par \par \uc2 ''We are in Italy here,'' said Roberto Del Savio, a union representative and an employee at the base. ''There are precise rules and thank God for that.''\par \par \uc2 Aviano, an Italian air base that hosts the United States 31st Fighter Wing, employs more than 700 Italian civilian personnel who on a daily basis cook and clean and generally keep the base running.\par \par \uc2 In all about 4,000 Italian civilian employees work at bases serving about 15,000 American soldiers in Italy, turning each into a sort of a miniature American town where U.S. military personnel can find American food and other familiar items from home.\par \par \uc2 Those jobs, in keeping with longstanding labor traditions in Italy, are fully unionized and protected under Italian labor laws. But at the same time, the employees work for the \uc2 United States government\uc2 , which pays their salaries.\par \par \uc2 Labor unions say the email was forwarded from a department head to dozens of Italian civilian employees working in the Aviano base's Army & Air Force exchange service, which provides goods and services to the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 No one seemed certain whether it was a one-off misunderstanding or if Mr. Musk was attempting to assert his demands over Italian workers as well as American ones. A \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  official said that while those emails were meant for U.S. employees, local employees ''could receive emails,'' too.\par \par \uc2 The confusion raised questions of whether Mr. Musk could export his brand of unbridled techno-libertarianism to a country that is ''founded on labor'' per the first article of its Constitution, or whether his chain-saw would snag on Italy's notoriously thick bureaucracy.\par \par \uc2 ''Ours is a system built on democracy, safeguards, and protections provided by contracts that must be respected,'' Pierpaolo Bombardieri, the secretary general of Italy's \uc2 Uil union\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bombardieri called the emails ''unacceptable'' and the method ''aberrant.'' Italy's unions wrote to the Italian government and the U.S. Embassy asking for explanations.\par \par \uc2 For now, the ground rule appears to be that Italian civilians must answer the email only if they receive it directly from the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  -- not if it is forwarded to them, as happened at Aviano and at least one other base in Italy, in the city of Vicenza. But it remained unclear whether the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  was going to reach out to Italian workers directly.\par \par \uc2 Some German employees of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  in Germany also received Mr. Musk's first email asking them to explain their work output, said a senior diplomat in Berlin, who did not want to be named while talking about an ally. (Mr. Musk's follow-up email appears to have been sent only to American employees in Germany, the diplomat said.)\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, some Italian employees had answered the email, said Mr. Del Savio. ''One says I was slicing pizza, another says something else.'' he said. ''But we were all very puzzled,'' he said. ''Italy is not the Wild West like the U.S.''\par \par \uc2 Despite recent changes that attempted to make the labor market more flexible, Italy's labor laws continue to offer broad protections to employees. Especially in the public sector, getting a permanent job is often seen as a guarantee to be unfireable for life.\par \par \uc2 Many in Italy value this system as a backbone of the Italian welfare state and its democracy, while others point to it as a rigid and inefficient juggernaut that prevents jobs from being created for young people.\par \par \uc2 Stories of half-hour long workdays and daylong coffee breaks are something of a legend in Italy. Some have said a touch of Musk-style slash and burn approach would not hurt here.\par \par \uc2 ''Italy would also need Musk's ax,'' Nicola Porro, an Italian journalist and right-wing commentator, wrote in a blog post, decrying Italy's ''useless positions.''\par \par \uc2 Italians seized upon the juxtaposition. One TikTok creator, Alberico Di Pasquale, made a video pretending to show an Italian employee on a permanent contract answering Mr. Musk's email. ''No. 1: I come to work, No. 2: I clock in, No. 3: breakfast,'' he said. ''No. 4: tournament with my colleagues to see who will get the coffee; No. 5: I get the coffee. Repeat five times points 4 and 5. No. 6: I go pay my bills and grocery shop; No. 7, I clock out.''\par \par \uc2 But while some had fun with the demands from Mr. Musk, for union representatives at the American base in Aviano, and other Italians, it was serious business.\par \par \uc2 As Mr. Trump questions the U.S. commitment to \uc2 NATO\uc2  and insists that Europe must defend itself, fears of spending cuts are spreading at U.S. bases abroad.\par \par \uc2 Amid a 30-day freeze of federal credit cards, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  last week also froze the credit cards that Italian employees at Aviano used to purchase equipment for the base, then started a hiring freeze, the unions said.\par \par \uc2 Union workers said they did not know what was going to come next. But they said they were going to fight on.\par \par \uc2 ''Musk can do whatever he wants in the United States,'' said Emilio Fargnoli, a union representative. ''If they are happy with it, sure,'' he added. ''Not here.''\par \par \uc2 Jim Tankersley\uc2  and Jeanna Smialek contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Jim Tankersley\uc2  and Jeanna Smialek contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250316el3g0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
