\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Aid Agency's Employees Are Ordered to Shred or Burn Sensitive Documents\b0\par\par\uc2 By Edward Wong \par \uc2 1,053 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 March 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Union representatives demanded that the aid agency follow the law stated in the Federal Records Act. Defense lawyers argued that officials had not destroyed personnel records and would not destroy any more documents without notification.\par \par \uc2 A senior official at the main U.S. aid agency, which is being dismantled by the Trump administration, told employees to clear safes holding classified documents and personnel files by shredding the papers or putting them into bags for burning, according to an email sent to the staff.\par \par \uc2 The email, sent by Erica Y. Carr, the acting executive secretary, told employees of the \uc2 U.S. Agency for International Development\uc2  to empty out the classified safes and personnel document files on Tuesday. ''Shred as many documents first, and reserve the burn bags for when the shredder becomes unavailable or needs a break,'' Ms. Carr wrote, according to a copy of the email obtained by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The agency has fired thousands of employees, put some on paid leave and asked a few to work from home, so its headquarters have been mostly empty for weeks.\par \par \uc2 It is unclear if Ms. Carr or any other official at \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  got permission from the \uc2 National Archives and Records Administration\uc2  to destroy the documents. The Federal Records Act of 1950 requires \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  officials to ask the records administration for approval before destroying documents.\par \par \uc2 The documents being destroyed could have relevance to multiple court cases that have been filed against the Trump administration and the aid agency over the mass firing and sudden relocation of employees, the rapid dismantlement of the agency and a freeze on almost all foreign aid money.\par \par \uc2 The State Department and a spokesperson for \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 By Tuesday evening, at least two groups had made court filings to try to get judges to prevent the destruction of more documents at \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  They said the agency had failed to comply with record-keeping requirements. Defense lawyers argued in a filing that officials had not destroyed any personnel records and that they would not destroy any more documents in the Ronald Reagan Building, the site of the agency's headquarters, without first notifying the plaintiff and the court.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Foreign Service Association\uc2 , which is a union representing career diplomats and a plaintiff, said in a statement on Tuesday that it was ''alarmed by reports that \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  has directed the destruction of classified and sensitive documents that may be relevant to ongoing litigation regarding the termination of \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  employees and the cessation of \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  grants.''\par \par \uc2 ''Federal law is clear: The preservation of government records is essential to transparency, accountability and the integrity of the legal process,'' the union said. ''We call for full adherence to federal records preservation laws to ensure accountability and protect the rights of \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  employees.''\par \par \uc2 The union also noted that employees engaged in the improper destruction of records could find themselves in legal jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 The aid agency employs nearly 2,000 career diplomats, known as Foreign Service officers, and they are represented by the union. Diplomats generally destroy large numbers of documents only when an embassy or other post is about to be overrun by a hostile force. Some diplomats who got Ms. Carr's email on Monday night called union officials after becoming anxious over the sudden request.\par \par \uc2 The Federal Records Act says that ''agencies must follow retention schedules approved by'' the records agency and covers all electronic documents as well. Printed documents must be saved in an electronic format before being destroyed, and the act says that ''agencies must manage electronic records effectively, ensuring accessibility and security.''\par \par \uc2 In some federal agencies, employees regularly destroy physical documents after saving them in electronic form. It is unclear if employees at \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  have ensured that all physical documents are being saved electronically before being destroyed.\par \par \uc2 In her email on Monday, Ms. Carr asked employees to meet at 9:30 a.m. on Tuesday in the lobby of the Reagan Building to take part in the destruction of documents. The \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  has ended the agency's lease for office space there. Secretary of State \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , who is the agency's acting administrator, plans to move the remnants of \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  into the State Department after firing thousands more employees.\par \par \uc2 The email told employees to mark burn bags with ''SECRET'' or ''\uc2 USAID\uc2 /(B/IO)'' with a dark Sharpie if possible. ''B/IO'' stands for bureau or independent office.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rubio took charge of \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  last month and announced that Pete Marocco, a divisive appointee at the State Department, would oversee day-to-day operations. Mr. Marocco has worked with young employees of a task force run by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the billionaire adviser to President Trump, to halt the disbursement of foreign aid funds, cut contracts and fire thousands of employees or put them on leave.\par \par \uc2 In a volatile cabinet meeting at the White House last Thursday, Mr. Rubio vented his anger at Mr. Musk for the eradication of the aid agency.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Mr. Rubio announced on social media that officials had canceled 5,200 aid agency contracts, or 83 percent of the total. The remaining 1,000 will be managed by the State Department, he said. And he thanked Mr. Musk's team.\par \par \uc2 The State Department has not released details of the remaining 1,000 contracts.\par \par \uc2 Several hundred employees of \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  will probably be absorbed into the State Department, aid agency officials say.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge ordered the Trump administration on Monday to restart aid payments for work that had been completed. The judge noted that Congress had appropriated foreign aid funds, and that the Trump administration did not have the right to ''impound'' the money. An executive order signed by Mr. Trump on Jan. 20 froze almost all foreign aid funding.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Mac contributed reporting from Los Angeles and Seamus Hughes contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Mac contributed reporting from Los Angeles and Seamus Hughes contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250312el3c00044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
