\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Leader of \uc2 Nissan\uc2  to Step Down Amid Restructuring and Strains\b0\par\par\uc2 By River Akira Davis \par \uc2 785 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 March 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The automaker said on Tuesday that \uc2 Makoto Uchida\uc2  would step down. The company has said it would slash global production capacity and cut thousands of jobs.\par \par \uc2 Nissan Motor\uc2  is tapping a new chief executive as it grapples with a growing list of setbacks, from sluggish sales to failed merger talks and looming tariff threats in the United States.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the Japanese automaker announced that \uc2 Makoto Uchida\uc2 , its chief executive since 2019, would step down. Ivan Espinosa, 46, a \uc2 Nissan\uc2  veteran of two decades and the company's chief planning officer, will take over as leader next month, the company said.\par \par \uc2 The move follows a tumultuous year for \uc2 Nissan\uc2 , putting the nearly century-old company into a leadership transition midway through a restructuring effort and at a time of heightened uncertainty for the industry.\par \par \uc2 Yasushi Kimura\uc2 , the chair of \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's board of directors, said in a news conference on Tuesday that it would be ''a very challenging start'' for Mr. Espinosa when he stepped into the top job on April 1. But ''given the industrywide challenges and \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's performance, we believe it is necessary and appropriate to change the top management team,'' Mr. Kimura said.\par \par \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's current leader, Mr. Uchida, 58, took charge during another turbulent period, after the ouster of \uc2 Carlos Ghosn\uc2 , then \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's chairman, on allegations of financial misconduct. \uc2 Nissan\uc2  was reeling from a sharp decline in profits, and its leadership was in disarray. Mr. Uchida stepped up after Mr. Ghosn's immediate successor resigned over separate issues involving his pay.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Uchida, who ran Nissan's China business before taking the helm of the group, overhauled its decades-old alliance with the French automaker \uc2 Renault\uc2  and embarked on a mission to boost profitability by scaling back incentives that had propped up sales during Mr. Ghosn's tenure.\par \par \uc2 For a time, his strategy seemed to be working. \uc2 Nissan\uc2  reported healthy profits in 2022 and 2023, buoyed by a postpandemic surge in demand and a favorable exchange rate. But the company's aging portfolio of models struggled to keep up with shifting consumer preferences for hybrid and fully electric vehicles in some of its core markets.\par \par \uc2 That has been especially true in China, where local E.V.-wielding champions are increasingly outselling smaller foreign brands. \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's unit sales in China fell more than 9 percent in the nine months through December. Struggling in other regions as well, \uc2 Nissan\uc2  cut its profit outlook three times in the current fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 In November, Mr. Uchida revealed restructuring plans that involved slashing \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's global production capacity and cutting thousands of jobs. At the time, he said he felt responsible for the company's failure to adapt to a fast-evolving market and volunteered to take a 50 percent pay cut.\par \par \uc2 Late last year, a potential lifeline emerged for \uc2 Nissan\uc2 : the possibility of merging with \uc2 Honda\uc2 . The combined entity would have been one of the largest auto groups in the world. But less than two months after the companies began talks, the discussions collapsed because \uc2 Nissan\uc2  opposed a proposal from \uc2 Honda\uc2  that \uc2 Nissan\uc2  become its subsidiary.\par \par \uc2 The announcement last month that the merger talks had failed was made on the same day that \uc2 Nissan\uc2  reported declining sales and a nearly 90 percent plunge in operating profit, to $435 million, for the nine months ending in December.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Uchida said on Tuesday that he had tried to improve \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's performance, but people inside and outside of the automaker had begun to question his role in the company's lack of progress.\par \par \uc2 ''I deeply regret that I had to pass the baton to my successor in these circumstances,'' Mr. Uchida said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Espinosa will confront not only the company's existing problems but also new and potentially more challenging ones. Top among those threats are President Trump's proposed tariffs on cars imported from Japan, Canada and Mexico.\par \par \uc2 North America is by far \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's largest market, and roughly a third of the nearly one million vehicles it sold in the United States last year were built in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 During a hastily arranged news conference on Tuesday, Mr. Espinosa said little about how he planned to revive the automaker, saying repeatedly that he had just been informed of the board's decision to appoint him as \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's next chief.\par \par \uc2 What Mr. Espinosa does know, he said, ''is that \uc2 Nissan\uc2  has so much more potential than what we are seeing today.'' Asked about a potential reopening of merger talks with \uc2 Honda\uc2 , he declined to comment, saying instead that his focus was ''getting \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's teams engaged.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250312el3c0003o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
