\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Fired, Then Rehired, By the Trump Administration\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elena Shao and Ashley Wu \par \uc2 868 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Even as the Trump administration continues to slash federal jobs, a number of federal agencies have begun to reverse course -- reinstating some workers and pausing plans to dismiss others, sometimes within days of the firings. The Office of Personnel Management on Tuesday revised earlier guidance calling for probationary workers to be terminated, adding a disclaimer that agencies would have the final authority over personnel actions. It is unclear how many more workers could be reinstated as a result. Here's a look at some of the back-and-forths so far.\par \par \uc2 Rehiring Some Essential Workers\par \par \uc2 Trump-appointed officials fired, then scrambled to rehire some employees in critical jobs.\par \par \uc2 Workers Reviewing Food Safety and Medical Devices\par \par \uc2 AROUND FEB. 15 The Food and Drug Administration fired about 700 probationary employees, many of whom were not paid through taxpayer money.\par \par \uc2 ABOUT A WEEK LATER The agency reinstated dozens of employees, particularly those involved in food safety and review of medical devices.\par \par \uc2 Workers Involved in Bird Flu Response\par \par \uc2 AROUND FEB. 14 The Agricultre Department continued plans to fire thousands of employees, including hundreds in a plant and animal inspection program.\par \par \uc2 DAYS LATER The agency said it was trying to reverse the firings of some employees involved in responding to the nation's growing bird flu outbreak.\par \par \uc2 Workers Who Maintain the U.S. Nuclear Arsenal\par \par \uc2 FEB. 13 The Energy Department began laying off 1,000 of its probationary employees, including more than 300 who worked at the \uc2 National Nuclear Security Administration\uc2 , which maintains and secures the country's nuclear warheads. A spokesperson for the Energy Department disputed that number, saying fewer than 50 at the N.N.S.A. were fired.\par \par \uc2 ONE DAY LATER At least some of the dismissed staff members at the N.N.S.A. were told to come back.\par \par \uc2 Rehired After Political Pushback\par \par \uc2 Public opposition has also resulted in some fired workers getting called back.\par \par \uc2 Workers Managing a 9/11 Survivors' Health Program\par \par \uc2 AROUND FEB. 15 \uc2 The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  cut hundreds of employees, including 16 probationary workers who manage the World Trade Center Health Program, which administers aid to people who were exposed to hazards from the terrorist attacks on Sept. 11, 2001.\par \par \uc2 SEVERAL DAYS LATER After bipartisan pushback, the Trump administration said that fired employees would return to their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Scientific Researchers, Including Military Veterans\par \par \uc2 FEB. 18 The \uc2 National Science Foundation\uc2  fired 168 employees, or roughly 10 percent of its work force.\par \par \uc2 LESS THAN TWO WEEKS LATER The foundation began reversing dismissals of 84 probationary employees, in response to a ruling by a federal judge and guidance from the Office of Personnel Management to retain the employment of military veterans and military spouses.\par \par \uc2 Temporary Reinstatements and Pauses on Firings\par \par \uc2 The firing spree has prompted a slew of lawsuits, which have resulted in some temporary reversals.\par \par \uc2 Employees at a Consumer Watchdog Organization\par \par \uc2 FEB. 11 Officials fired almost 200 employees at the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2 , a financial industry watchdog, and ordered the rest to stop their work.\par \par \uc2 DAYS LATER A federal judge ordered a temporary halt to firings.\par \par \uc2 Employees at an International Aid Department\par \par \uc2 FEB. 6 The Trump administration said it would reduce the number of \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  workers from more than 10,000 to about 290.\par \par \uc2 A DAY LATER A federal judge ordered the Trump administration to temporarily halt the layoffs.\par \par \uc2 TWO WEEKS LATER The judge ruled that the administration could proceed with plans to lay off or put on paid leave many agency employees. \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  moved to fire around 2,000 U.S.-based workers and put up to thousands of foreign service officers and others on paid leave.\par \par \uc2 Workers from multiple agencies have also filed complaints with the office of a government watchdog lawyer who himself has been targeted by Mr. Trump for termination. In response to requests from that office, an independent federal worker board has considered some of the claims and temporarily reinstated some workers.\par \par \uc2 Workers at the Agriculture Department\par \par \uc2 FEB. 13 The Agriculture Department began cutting thousands of jobs, including around 3,400 in the Forest Service.\par \par \uc2 THREE WEEKS LATER The \uc2 Merit Systems Protection Board\uc2  issued a stay ordering the department to reinstate fired workers while an investigation continued.\par \par \uc2 Six Workers From Six Federal Agencies\par \par \uc2 AROUND FEB. 13 The Office of Personnel Management ordered federal agencies to fire tens of thousands of federal employees.\par \par \uc2 LESS THAN TWO WEEKS LATER The \uc2 Merit Systems Protection Board\uc2  temporarily reinstated six fired federal workers from the Departments of Agriculture, Education, Energy, Housing and Urban Development and Veterans Affairs, and the Office of Personnel Management.\par \par \uc2 The back-and-forth and lack of transparency surrounding the administration's cost-cutting moves have deepened the confusion and alarm of federal workers, many of whom also have to interpret confusing email guidance and gauge the veracity of various circulating rumors.\par \par \uc2 ''The layoffs and then rehires undermine the productivity and confidence not only of the people who left and came back but of the people who stayed,'' said Stephen Goldsmith, an urban policy professor at \uc2 Harvard's Kennedy School\uc2  and a former mayor of Indianapolis.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250308el380004w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
