\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Veterans Are Bearing Brunt of Trump's Overhaul of the Federal Work Force\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eileen Sullivan and Maya C. Miller \par \uc2 1,508 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 March 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nearly 30 percent of civil service employees in the federal government are veterans. Democrats are highlighting their firings by taking some as guests to President Trump's address on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Veterans, who make up a disproportionate share of federal employees, are feeling the brunt of the Trump administration's rapid push to downsize the work force, generating discord in a reliable political base for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Some Republican lawmakers are facing backlash at town hall gatherings where people have raised concerns about veterans being part of the deep cuts made by President Trump and his partner in this mission, the tech billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And in an act of protest, some Democrats are bringing fired veterans as their guests to Mr. Trump's address to a joint session of Congress on Tuesday, where he is expected to outline more of his agenda.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 30 percent of civil service employees in the federal government are veterans, according to data as of September from the Office of Personnel Management, the government's human resources arm.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump and Mr. Musk have fired more than 20,000 employees who were in probationary status, based on data compiled by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . About 6,000 of all fired federal employees so far were veterans, according to a count by Democrats on the \uc2 House Appropriations Committee\uc2  as of Feb. 23.\par \par \uc2 This includes more than 2,000 veterans at the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2 , 676 at Treasury, about 500 at the Energy Department and 450 at the Agriculture Department.\par \par \uc2 More job losses are around the corner as agencies begin a formal process known as reduction in force, including at the Pentagon, where veterans make up 43 percent of the civilian work force, according to data from the Office of Personnel Management.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for the agency said it was aware that restructuring could be difficult for the employees who were fired.\par \par \uc2 ''O.P.M. recognizes the dedication and service of all federal employees, including our veteran work force, and remains committed to supporting them during this transition,'' the spokeswoman, McLaurine Pinover, said in a statement on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Veterans who have been fired are speaking out.\par \par \uc2 Jacob Bushno, a veteran who did two tours in Iraq while he was in the Army's air assault division, was fired seven days shy of completing his one-year probationary period at the Forest Service.\par \par \uc2 ''My mental health has been a roller coaster since this happened,'' Mr. Bushno, who worked in the Shawnee National Forest in Illinois, said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bushno said his performance review at the end of September was positive. Yet, like thousands of other fired federal employees less than a year into their positions, his termination letter said he did not meet the agency's standard and demonstrated poor performance. He is challenging his firing with an appeal to the \uc2 Merit Systems Protection Board\uc2 , an independent agency that reviews personnel actions.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bushno has also appealed to his congressman, Representative \uc2 Mike Bost\uc2 , Republican of Illinois and the chairman of the \uc2 House Veterans Affairs Committee\uc2 . Mr. Bushno said he had yet to receive a response from Mr. Bost, whose office did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 ''You know, they say they're patriotic,'' Mr. Bushno said of Mr. Bost and other Republicans who had been quiet on the issue. ''I haven't seen any patriotism out of them since all this has been going down.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bushno said he had reached out to the office of Senator Tammy Duckworth, Democrat of Illinois, and heard back from someone the next day.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, out of power in Washington, are pushing back on Mr. Trump's rapid culling of the federal work force, including putting Republicans on the defensive over the firings that have hit veterans hard.\par \par \uc2 Representative Tom Barrett, Republican of Michigan, sent a letter on Tuesday to \uc2 Doug Collins\uc2 , the secretary of veterans affairs, writing that he supported the president's goal of reducing the size of the federal bureaucracy but that he was ''concerned that probationary but otherwise qualified veterans are being unintentionally let go in the process.''\par \par \uc2 Representative Mark Alford, Republican of Missouri, faced angry constituents at a town hall on Feb. 25 as he tried to assure them that he supported veterans.\par \par \uc2 ''You do not care about us as federal employees,'' Rebecca Reinholdt, who identified herself as the vice president of the local \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2  union in Leavenworth, Kan., shouted at him.\par \par \uc2 Senator Roger Marshall, Republican of Kansas, was booed at a town hall on Saturday when people started criticizing the firing of veterans. Mr. Marshall left early.\par \par \uc2 And at a news conference last week, a chairman of the House DOGE caucus, Representative Aaron Bean, Republican of Florida, said he knew veterans were hard workers and was confident they would find other jobs. (DOGE is the acronym for the Department of Government Efficiency, the group established by Mr. Trump and led by Mr. Musk that has directed the job cuts.)\par \par \uc2 ''And if anybody can overcome obstacles of getting another job, it's our veterans,'' Mr. Bean said.\par \par \uc2 Alina Habba, a counselor to the president, told reporters inquiring about the status of fired veterans that the Trump administration would ''care for them in the right way.''\par \par \uc2 But, she said, ''we have a fiscal responsibility to use taxpayer dollars to pay people that actually work.'' She suggested that ''perhaps they're not fit to have a job at this moment or not willing to come to work.''\par \par \uc2 Neither the White House nor the Department of Veterans Affairs responded to questions about whether officials had found examples of veterans who were not fit to work or did not show up to their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump said he was aware of the veterans affected by the firings.\par \par \uc2 ''We take good care of our veterans, so we're watching that very carefully, and we hope it's going to be as small a number as possible,'' he said on Thursday in the Oval Office. ''But we are having great success at slimming down our government.''\par \par \uc2 Senator Tim Kaine, Democrat of Virginia, said Mr. Trump had waged a ''war'' on veterans with the firings.\par \par \uc2 ''And I'm calling it a war on veterans, because I don't think it can be just chalked up to accidental when the people making these decisions know who the federal work force is,'' the senator said in an interview on Monday.\par \par \uc2 If Democrats were vocal in opposing the cuts, Mr. Kaine said, he believed this was one area where Mr. Trump could back down.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kaine said that was why many Democrats, including him, were bringing fired veterans to Mr. Trump's address to the joint session of Congress.\par \par \uc2 ''We're going to continue to really push this and get louder and louder,'' said Mr. Kaine, whose guest will be a disabled veteran from Fairfax, Va., who was fired from the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2 's safety division.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2 , Democrat of Arizona, is bringing a veteran from North Phoenix who was recently fired from his national security job at the \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  of New York, the minority leader, is hosting an Army veteran, Alissa Ellman, who was recently fired from the \uc2 Veterans Benefits Administration\uc2  in Buffalo, where she helped veterans with education claims.\par \par \uc2 ''If this is really about government efficiency, then you're doing a really bad job when you fire people who are working really hard,'' said Ms. Ellman, who was deployed to Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Representative Jen Kiggans, Republican of Virginia, was one of few Republicans to speak publicly against the cuts. She sent a letter to Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth on Monday urging him to prioritize veterans when making future cuts, including finding other federal positions for them.\par \par \uc2 ''Many of these veterans already experience the daily challenges of transitioning to civilian life, and facing such layoffs only add to their undue burden,'' wrote Ms. Kiggans, whose district includes Hampton Roads, Va., where many veterans are working in civilian jobs.\par \par \uc2 The federal government has prioritized hiring veterans and streamlined the process at the beginning of the Obama administration. Toward the end of his first administration, Mr. Trump issued an executive order directing agencies to hire a work force based on skills and not on where the applicant received a college degree.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration continued the practice, said William G. Resh, a public policy professor at the \uc2 University of Southern California\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As a result, many of the probationary employees who had been fired were hired on merit, just as Mr. Trump called for in 2020, Mr. Resh said.\par \par \uc2 On its website, the Office of Personnel Management promoted the value of hiring veterans. ''We recognize those who have served in our armed forces often have the experience, skills and dedication necessary to achieve success as members of our civilian work force,'' it said, ''and it is critical that we harness that talent.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250305el350003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
