\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Shift in Media As \uc2 Paramount\uc2  Is Scaling Back D.E.I. Policies\b0\par\par\uc2 By Benjamin Mullin \par \uc2 457 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The company has a merger with \uc2 Skydance\uc2  pending before the \uc2 Federal Communications Commission\uc2 , whose chief has criticized diversity, equity and inclusion hiring programs.\par \par \uc2 Paramount\uc2 , the parent company of BET, \uc2 MTV\uc2  and the Paramount film studio, said on Wednesday that it was rethinking its approach to diversity, equity and inclusion amid a broader backlash toward D.E.I. policies driven by the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 In a note to employees obtained by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , \uc2 Paramount\uc2 's co-chief executives said they were making changes to the company's D.E.I. policies to conform to efforts by the Trump administration to eliminate diversity programs in the federal government and the private sector.\par \par \uc2 The administration's policies, the note said, ''require changes in the way the company approaches inclusion moving forward.''\par \par \uc2 Paramount\uc2  is ending its practices of using aspirational hiring goals related to race, ethnicity, sex or gender, said the memo, which was sent by the co-chief executives \uc2 Brian Robbins\uc2 , \uc2 George Cheeks\uc2  and \uc2 Chris McCarthy\uc2 . The company will also stop collecting gender and diversity data for many U.S. job applicants and eliminate the D.E.I. component of its employee incentive plan.\par \par \uc2 ''With our business objectives firmly in mind, we will continue to evaluate our programs and approach to ensure that we are widening our aperture to attract talent from all geographies, backgrounds and perspectives,'' the memo said. ''That may mean expanding existing programs while ending others.''\par \par \uc2 Paramount\uc2 's pronouncement on Wednesday makes it the latest company to rethink its approach to D.E.I. since President Trump took office in January. Major companies including Target, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 Disney\uc2  have made similar changes to their diversity and inclusion initiatives.\par \par \uc2 Paramount\uc2  has a multibillion-dollar merger with the Hollywood studio Skydance pending review by the \uc2 Federal Communications Commission\uc2 , whose chairman, \uc2 Brendan Carr\uc2 , has been critical of D.E.I. policies. Mr. Carr said in a letter to \uc2 Comcast\uc2  this month that he was investigating such policies at the cable giant with an eye toward eliminating ''invidious forms of discrimination'' across the media sector.\par \par \uc2 Paramount\uc2  has already begun scrubbing examples of D.E.I. from its public-facing statements. Its D.E.I. page, with the header ''Global Inclusion,'' no longer includes any mention of that initialism, and the company is eliminating the phrase from its annual financial filing. An archived version of Paramount's D.E.I. website from May underscores the extent of the company's about-face.\par \par \uc2 ''We embed D.E.I. into every aspect of our employee experience,'' the page read. ''Including specific programs to strengthen how we attract, develop, retain and measure our progress.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250228el2s0000x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
