\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 New Phase Begins in Cuts To the Federal Work Force\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eileen Sullivan \par \uc2 1,081 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers at a handful of agencies have begun receiving notifications that they are part of the ''reduction in force'' directed by the president, after a wave of firings that targeted probationary workers.\par \par \uc2 The next phase in the Trump administration's effort to rapidly downsize the federal work force was well underway on Tuesday as confusion roiled through the ranks over how employees should respond to an ultimatum from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to justify their roles or be fired.\par \par \uc2 Since Sunday afternoon, at least three independent agencies have formally alerted staff members of upcoming dismissals through a ''reduction in force'' process ordered by President Trump, according to emails shared with \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and employees who spoke on the condition of anonymity out of fear of reprisal.\par \par \uc2 That process, part of Mr. Trump and Mr. Musk's large-scale plan to make drastic cuts to the number of federal employees across the country, follows the firing of thousands of probationary workers. Mr. Musk, the tech billionaire, is Mr. Trump's right-hand man in this extraordinary effort.\par \par \uc2 Reductions in force typically mean a significant elimination of roles as part of a restructuring at the agency.\par \par \uc2 So far, the early ''reduction in force'' round includes cutting 144 people at the \uc2 Department of Housing and Urban Development\uc2  in offices across the country. The \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2  has plans to cut 30 to 40 people, with notifications going out to individuals by the end of the week. And on Sunday, the \uc2 U.S. Agency for International Development\uc2  notified its staff that some 2,000 employees based in the United States would be cut.\par \par \uc2 ''I regret to inform you that you are affected by a reduction in force (RIF),'' the aid agency's acting director, Peter Marocco, wrote in a standard letter to the employees affected by the cuts. ''This RIF action does not reflect directly on your service, performance or conduct.'' Mr. Marocco said it was part of an agency restructuring to ''better reflect'' the priorities of \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  and foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 Scott Turner, the housing secretary, wrote in a memo to an employee that ''a reduction in force within your competitive area will be conducted effective March 28.'' The Times obtained a copy of the memo with the employee's name redacted.\par \par \uc2 Don Moynihan, a public policy professor at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , said the use of reductions in force can be complicated. In a social media post, Mr. Moynihan predicted two fights: whether agencies followed the legal process and whether the courts agree that Mr. Trump can ''RIF'' agencies funded consistently by Congress.\par \par \uc2 In an executive order this month, Mr. Trump directed agencies to prepare for a ''large scale'' reduction in force.\par \par \uc2 Five labor unions representing federal workers sued the Trump administration this month over its plans to use that path to fire large swaths of the federal work force. The unions say the administration's use of the practice violates protocol set by Congress. A federal judge in the District of Columbia denied the unions' request for a temporary pause in the firings and directed the unions to make their argument to the \uc2 Federal Labor Relations Authority\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This stage of cuts follows a confusing directive from the Office of Personnel Management on Monday night, instructing employees to respond to an equally confounding request initiated by Mr. Musk over the weekend. On social media, he announced that by the end of the day on Monday, all federal employees were to provide five bullet points on what they had accomplished the previous week. Not long after Mr. Musk's announcement, the personnel office sent an email with the subject line ''What did you do last week?''\par \par \uc2 Some agencies directed their employees not to respond to the email at all. Then on Monday, less than four hours from the response deadline, the personnel office announced that all employees should reply.\par \par \uc2 This posed challenges to employees who did not have access to their email over the weekend or on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Some employees at the Department of Veterans Affairs do not routinely log into their government email, as it is not part of their jobs, and do not have a government-issued computer. On Monday, a human resources official with the \uc2 Veterans Health Administration\uc2  in Portland, Ore., announced that five computers would be set up in the lobby of its building until 9 p.m. so that employees could respond, according to an email obtained by The Times.\par \par \uc2 It was not immediately clear on Tuesday whether employees had been fired for not responding with a list of five accomplishments. In the last-minute instructions from the personnel office, managers were told they could make those decisions themselves.\par \par \uc2 The probationary firings and the reduction-in-force effort also appear to have collided in some cases, causing additional confusion and turmoil.\par \par \uc2 In at least one case, an employee at \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  said they were fired two different ways: once with a notification that the employee was part of a reduction-in-force plan, and again because the employee is probationary. The employee declined to speak publicly out of concerns about retribution.\par \par \uc2 ''They are making sloppy mistakes,'' said \uc2 Kevin Owen\uc2 , an employment lawyer with Gilbert Employment Law, referring to the Trump team leading the effort. ''They don't know what they're doing to the level of professionalism as we would expect federal H.R. employee professionals to be advising.''\par \par \uc2 On Monday evening, in an email announcing the upcoming cuts that did not offer much detail, the acting administrator of the \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2 , Stephen Ehikian, said the agency was seeking approval from the personnel office to lower the required age and length of service to be eligible for voluntary early retirement.\par \par \uc2 ''I offer my sincere and heartfelt gratitude for all G.S.A. employees impacted by this decision,'' Mr. Ehikian wrote. ''I appreciate your service to this nation, as well as my best wishes for the next stages of your lives and careers.''\par \par \uc2 Between 30 and 40 employees would be targeted in this first round of reduction-in-force cuts, according to a person familiar with the plan who was not authorized to speak about it publicly.\par \par \uc2 The person said those notifications should go out by the end of this week.\par \par \uc2 Claire Cain Miller contributed reporting from Portland, Ore.\par \par \uc2 Claire Cain Miller contributed reporting from Portland, Ore.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250227el2r0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
