\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Hundreds of Park Service Workers Accept Offer to Resign\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Friedman \par \uc2 476 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The report has raised concerns about national park access and maintenance heading into the summer travel season.\par \par \uc2 More than 700 \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  employees have submitted resignations as part of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's ''fork in the road'' offer, according to an internal agency memo that critics of the plan said would diminish staffing ahead of the busy summer tourism season.\par \par \uc2 The news of the resignations comes after a decision earlier this month at the Department of Interior to fire more than 1,000 full-time national park employees. According to the new memo sent on Tuesday and viewed by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , the additional 700 workers who agreed to the resignation plan would not be permitted to work after March 7.\par \par \uc2 The staffing cuts have sparked a public outcry. Conservationists, outdoor enthusiasts and park rangers have warned that the reductions threaten to leave hundreds of national parks understaffed during the busy summer season, and already are causing some parks to reduce hours, cancel tours and close visitor centers.\par \par \uc2 The national park job losses are part of a chaotic effort by President Trump to delete thousands of federal jobs. Adding to the confusion, Interior Secretary \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  has said the park service also plans to rehire thousands of workers -- albeit as temporary, summer positions.\par \par \uc2 ''The \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  is hiring seasonal workers to continue enhancing the visitor experience as we embrace new opportunities for optimization and innovation in work force management,'' Elizabeth Peace, a spokeswoman for the Interior Department, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 ''We are focused on ensuring that every visitor has the chance to explore and connect with the incredible, iconic spaces of our national parks,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Kristen Brengel, the senior vice president of government affairs at the \uc2 National Parks Conservation Association\uc2 , a nonprofit group, has said the temporary positions are not a substitute for the employees with years of full-time experience now lost to the park system.\par \par \uc2 She also noted that about 2,000 prospective seasonal employees had their job offers rescinded when Mr. Trump, during his first days in office, imposed a hiring freeze across the government. That freeze compromised the ability to accelerate the process of rehiring those people.\par \par \uc2 During the warm-weather months, as many as 325 million people visit the nation's 63 national parks and hundreds of historic sites and other attractions managed by the park service.\par \par \uc2 Federal workers received the Trump administration's resignation offer in an email last month entitled ''A Fork in the Road.'' Under the offer, employees who accepted it would leave their jobs, but continue getting paid through September -- and those who did not accept it risked being fired. According to the Office of Personnel Management, about 75,000 workers across the government have accepted the offer.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250226el2q0004s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
