\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Lays Off 7% Of Workers At Corporate\b0\par\par\uc2 By Danielle Kaye \par \uc2 623 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The jobs cuts, which will affect about 7 percent of the chain's non-retail staff, are part of its new chief executive's plan to win back customers.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is laying off 1,100 corporate employees, its chief executive said in a letter to workers on Monday, marking the coffee chain's latest move to restructure amid lackluster sales.\par \par \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2 , who took over as chief executive last fall, has vowed to woo back customers who have shied away from $8 lattes and long wait times, which have put pressure on sales in recent months. Same-store sales globally slipped 4 percent in the first quarter of the company's 2025 fiscal year, which ended on Dec. 29. The layoffs, Mr. Niccol said in the letter, are aimed at allowing \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to ''operate more efficiently'' and ''reduce complexity.''\par \par \uc2 The cuts will affect nearly 7 percent of the company's 16,000 employees who work outside company-owned stores; baristas are not included. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had indicated in January that it planned to cut corporate jobs.\par \par \uc2 ''We believe it's a necessary change to position \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  for future success,'' Mr. Niccol said in his note. The company, he said, will also scrap several hundred open and unfilled positions.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  rose more than 1 percent on Monday morning.\par \par \uc2 The move comes amid Mr. Niccol's efforts to rethink the in-store experience for customers and streamline the business, part of his pledge to bring back the more personal coffeehouse atmosphere for which \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  was originally known. He has called for changes to the mobile ordering systems to address surges in orders, and has cut some items from the menu. The company also announced that it would not increase prices for the 2025 fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Mr. Niccol shook up the chain's leadership in North America, a market that accounts for about three-quarters of its revenue. Mike Grams, formerly the president and chief operating officer of \uc2 Taco Bell\uc2 , and Meredith Sandland, who was chief executive of Empower Delivery, a restaurant software company, were named to two new roles overseeing store performance, development and design.\par \par \uc2 ''We're on track to turn the business around,'' Mr. Niccol told Wall Street analysts and investors on an earnings call last month, his first since joining the company in September. ''But much of our work is just beginning.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Niccol was one of the highest-paid corporate executives last year. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  awarded Mr. Niccol $96 million in 2024 to make up for what he would have earned if he had stayed at Chipotle, where he previously was chief executive for six years.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Niccol also secured a commuting perk: \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  agreed to let him use its corporate plane to fly 1,000 miles from his home in Newport Beach, Calif., to the company's headquarters in Seattle. Mr. Niccol said on Monday that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  would start requiring employees at the vice president level and above to work from either its Seattle or Toronto offices three days a week.\par \par \uc2 Most \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  employees work in its company-operated stores. Mr. Niccol became chief executive amid a union drive among baristas in the United States that began in 2021; more than 500, or about 5 percent, of its company-owned U.S. locations have organized. The company and Workers United, the union that represents thousands of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers, agreed last month to bring in a mediator to revive contract talks, which had stalled over wage increases.\par \par \uc2 ''It's clearer than ever that baristas are central to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's turnaround,'' Michelle Eisen, a longtime \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  barista who has helped lead the organizing campaign, said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250225el2p0004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
