\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Civil Servants Using Any Levers They Can To Push Back on Musk\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Nehamas, Ryan Mac and Nikole Hannah-Jones \par \uc2 1,846 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 On Feb. 7, as rumors spread through the ranks of the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2  that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's team had entered their building, federal workers took out their phones.\par \par \uc2 On high alert, they filmed unidentified young men from the team known as the Department of Government Efficiency being escorted by security through the glass doors of their downtown Washington headquarters. They shouted greetings from afar and tried to snap photos of their faces. Once the men were inside, one agency worker even confronted them in a conference room, demanding to see their credentials, in an incident described to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . One of the Musk aides used his laptop to block his ID badge from view.\par \par \uc2 As Mr. Musk and his associates have swept rapidly through government agencies, dismantling programs and seizing access to sensitive databases, some federal employees are pushing back -- using whatever levers they have to resist the orders of the world's richest man, both in public and behind closed doors.\par \par \uc2 They have stepped down from their posts and filed more than two dozen lawsuits. They have staged protests outside the federal buildings that Mr. Musk's aides have penetrated and joined federal worker unions in droves. They have sent emails to hundreds of colleagues, blasting the new administration at the risk of their own livelihoods and careers. They have set up encrypted Signal chats, \uc2 Zoom\uc2  calls and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  accounts to share information and plan future actions.\par \par \uc2 During one video meeting with a representative of Mr. Musk's team, civil servants at the technology arm of the \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2  even bombarded an online chat with spoon emojis to express their displeasure at the deferred resignation offer known as the ''fork in the road.'' (Their bosses responded by removing spoons from the list of searchable emojis permitted in their videoconferencing platform.)\par \par \uc2 ''People are angry, they are frustrated, they are upset,'' said \uc2 Everett Kelley\uc2 , the president of the \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2 , the largest federal employee union. ''These are very patriotic people that actually care.''\par \par \uc2 So far, President Trump and his administration have largely steamrolled the opposition. Newly appointed Trump officials have moved to fire most of the 200,000 federal government employees on probation, with plans for what the president has hailed as ''large scale'' cuts to come. The administration has seized on creative loopholes to continue to bottle up government spending, despite court orders pausing those efforts. And Mr. Musk has spread misinformation about the work of the federal bureaucracy.\par \par \uc2 ''Anyone who thinks protests, lawsuits and lawfare will deter President Trump must have been sleeping under a rock for the past several years,'' the White House press secretary, Karoline Leavitt, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have struggled to identify a strategy to fight back, still reeling from a decisive loss in November that handed the White House and both houses of Congress to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 By banding together, federal workers say they hope to catalyze a wider movement. On balance, more Americans so far disapprove than approve of Mr. Musk's work with the federal government, although roughly 16 percent are not sure or did not offer an opinion, a new \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 /\uc2 Ipsos\uc2  poll found.\par \par \uc2 ''I want my colleagues who still have jobs to hang in there,'' said Hanna Hickman, a former lawyer at the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2  who was laid off this month and now hopes that union lawsuits will prevent a full shutdown of the agency. ''I'm out of a job but hopefully they aren't, and it's important for people to understand that there are people who will fight back.''\par \par \uc2 The pushback has come with peril, as some federal officials who have refused to carry out orders have felt compelled to leave their jobs, including most recently a wave of prosecutors at the Justice Department and the U.S. attorney's office in Manhattan and the acting chief of the \uc2 Social Security Administration\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The White House has also limited the ability of federal workers to fight back by disrupting many of the avenues that they had previously relied on to address grievances. Mr. Trump has pushed out 19 inspectors general; tried to fire the chairwoman of the \uc2 Merit Systems Protection Board\uc2 , which shields civil servants from unjustified disciplinary action; and dismissed the head of the \uc2 Office of Special Counsel\uc2 , an independent agency charged with safeguarding government whistle-blowers.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a deterrent to lawful whistle-blowing,'' said Mark Zaid, a lawyer who represents individuals who speak out about wrongdoing in the government. ''The pathetic irony is that it's encouraging people to break the law and leak classified information because the system is no longer in place.''\par \par \uc2 One of the agencies Mr. Trump has targeted is the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2 , which protects federal workers from discrimination and enforces federal anti-discrimination laws in the private sector.\par \par \uc2 Last month, a supervisor in the agency's New York District Office sent an email to several administrative judges ordering them to compile a list of all cases involving L.G.B.T.Q. discrimination in response to the president's executive order declaring that the United States will recognize only two sexes. The supervisor said no orders could be issued in those cases without first being reviewed by headquarters, according to a copy of the message viewed by The Times.\par \par \uc2 Karen Ortiz, one of those judges, responded by emailing the entire office, roughly 185 people, under the subject line ''THIS IS NOT NORMAL.''\par \par \uc2 ''Please RESIST. DO NOT COMPLY WITH THEIR ILLEGAL MANDATES,'' she wrote, referring to Mr. Trump's executive order. ''It's time for us to embody the civil rights work we were hired to do and honor the oath to the Constitution that we all took.''\par \par \uc2 To her surprise, her message generated no response. Soon, she realized that the email had been deleted from her mailbox -- and from those of her colleagues.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ortiz followed up with an agencywide message calling on the E.E.O.C.'s acting chair to resign. At that point, she said, her ability to send emails was shut off. She is still working, but has hired a team of lawyers.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ortiz said she had no regrets.\par \par \uc2 ''The unknown was not as scary as being complicit and not speaking up,'' Ms. Ortiz said in an interview with The Times. ''I can exist on cornflakes and community at the end of the day if it means that my soul is intact.''\par \par \uc2 The E.E.O.C. said in a statement that it had a ''longstanding policy prohibiting unauthorized all-employee emails.'' The acting chair, Andrea Lucas, said the commission ''robustly will comply with the president's executive orders, including the 'Defending Women From Gender Ideology Extremism and Restoring Biological Truth to the Federal Government' executive order.''\par \par \uc2 Some civil servants have also turned \uc2 Slack\uc2 , the workplace chatting app, into a weapon of resistance.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Slack\uc2  channel of the Technology Transformation Services -- the tech-focused arm of the \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2  -- has emerged as a forum for protest in recent weeks as employees have pushed back against moves by the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 This week, one T.T.S. employee used the channel to announce to his colleagues that he was resigning. The reason: He said he had been asked to grant Thomas Shedd, the newly appointed head of the group, access to the Notify.gov database, which is used by federal and state agencies to text the public about services.\par \par \uc2 The worker wrote that he saw it as a violation of his duties, adding that he believed granting Mr. Shedd such access could expose the personal information of Americans, including phone numbers, according to a message seen by The Times and reported earlier by the technology news website 404 Media.\par \par \uc2 His resignation spurred an outcry on the internal T.T.S. \uc2 Slack\uc2  channel, according to messages seen by The Times. Mr. Shedd responded by saying he had requested access to Notify.gov to ''ensure I have a detailed understanding of how the systems work.'' He said that he did not have administrative access ''at this time'' to the text database and that he was dismayed by the leaking of communications.\par \par \uc2 Will Powell, a \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2  spokesman, said that ''Mr. Shedd is working with all appropriate G.S.A. officials to ensure all established G.S.A. protocols and policies are followed before he is granted access to a T.T.S. system.''\par \par \uc2 Others across the government have found ways to voice their dissent internally.\par \par \uc2 Last week, employees inside a White House technology office that has been rebranded as Mr. Musk's cost-efficiency operation realized that a sign reading ''HATE HAS NO HOME HERE'' that they had hung in a window facing Lafayette Park had been removed, according to two people familiar with the situation. In response, they printed out more signs and hung them up -- some of which later disappeared again.\par \par \uc2 At the beleaguered \uc2 U.S. Agency for International Development\uc2 , a senior official last month countermanded an order that had placed dozens of staff members on administrative leave, returning them to active service. ''I wish you all the best,'' Nick Gottlieb, the agency's director of employee and labor relations, wrote in an email to them. ''You do not deserve this.'' Soon after, Mr. Gottlieb was himself put on leave. He did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 At the F.B.I., the top agent in its New York field office told his staff in an email that it was in ''the middle of a battle'' as Mr. Trump targeted officials involved in the investigations into the Jan. 6 attack.\par \par \uc2 Rank-and-file civil servants have responded to Mr. Musk's incursion by seeking the protections of unions. The \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2  said it had gained more than 20,000 members since Jan. 1, a significant increase compared with previous years. (In all of 2024, the union said it had added roughly 7,400 members.)\par \par \uc2 This month, hundreds of federal workers held a rally outside the Capitol, venting their anger at Mr. Musk with signs that said ''Fork Off, Elon'' and ''Launch Musk Into Orbit.'' Others have gathered outside the headquarters of \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  and the Office of Personnel Management. On Monday, thousands of people nationwide held Presidents' Day marches in state capitals, including Austin, Texas; Atlanta; Lansing, Mich.; and Sacramento, Calif.\par \par \uc2 ''We are used to being the punching bags of Congress,'' said Chris Dols, a dredging expert at the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  who has been participating in protests, speaking in his capacity as the president of a local chapter of the International Federation of Professional and Technical Engineers. ''But we are done being props.''\par \par \uc2 Kate Conger, Edward Wong and Ruth Igielnik contributed reporting. Kirsten Noyes and Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Kate Conger, Edward Wong and Ruth Igielnik contributed reporting. Kirsten Noyes and Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250223el2n0005d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
