\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 KFC\uc2  to Leave the State Of Its Fast-Food Birth\b0\par\par\uc2 By Victor Mather \par \uc2 709 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 KFC\uc2 , formerly named Kentucky Fried Chicken, disappointed state leaders when it announced it was moving to Texas.\par \par \uc2 KFC\uc2 , the company so associated with Kentucky that state flags flew at half-staff when its founder Colonel Sanders died, is leaving the state.\par \par \uc2 The company formerly known as Kentucky Fried Chicken will move its corporate headquarters and about 100 employees to Plano, Texas, from Louisville, Ky., its parent company, \uc2 Yum Brands\uc2 , said in a statement on Tuesday. \uc2 Yum Brands\uc2  also owns \uc2 Taco Bell\uc2 , Pizza Hut and the Habit Burger and Grill.\par \par \uc2 The move put an end to decades of the brand's history in the state, and drew condemnation from Kentucky's governor. While Yum cited ''greater collaboration among its brands'' as a reason for the move, Texas has also pushed aggressively in recent years to get companies to move there, promoting business-friendly policies like lower taxes and lighter regulation.\par \par \uc2 Yum Brands\uc2  and the KFC Foundation, a charitable group, will keep their corporate offices in Louisville, the company said. Pizza Hut is already based in Plano, and \uc2 Taco Bell\uc2  and Habit Burger and Grill are based in Irvine, Calif.\par \par \uc2 The company also said that it wanted its U.S.-based remote employees to return to the office where their work is based, and that it expected the process to take 18 months.\par \par \uc2 David Gibbs\uc2 , the chief executive officer of \uc2 Yum Brands\uc2 , said in a statement that bringing employees together would improve the company culture and hone talent.\par \par \uc2 But Kentucky officials lamented the loss of business.\par \par \uc2 ''I am disappointed by this decision and believe the company's founder would be, too,'' Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  said in a statement. ''This company's name starts with Kentucky, and it has marketed our state's heritage and culture in the sale of its product. My hope is that the company will rethink moving Kentucky Fried Chicken employees out of Kentucky.''\par \par \uc2 Harland Sanders, known universally as ''Colonel Sanders,'' started selling chicken from a roadside motel in 1930 in Kentucky. He prepared the meat in a pressure cooker, which sealed in the flavor. The secret chicken recipe, including its famous 11 herbs and spices, was established in 1939, and the colonel began wearing his trademark white suit in 1950. The first Kentucky Fried Chicken franchise opened in 1952 near Salt Lake City.\par \par \uc2 After selling the company in 1964, Mr. Sanders -- his title of ''colonel'' was honorary -- remained well known for his appearances in its commercials. Late in life, he was sometimes critical of changes made to the recipes by the company. But his likeness still dominates the logo.\par \par \uc2 When Mr. Sanders died in 1980, he lay in state at the Kentucky State Capitol in Frankfort. More than 1,000 people attended his funeral in freezing weather. Pat Boone sang, and the colonel was buried in his white suit.\par \par \uc2 The company said it would remain involved in Kentucky, including giving a $1 million endowment to the University of Louisville College of Business and building a flagship restaurant in that city.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky Fried Chicken shortened its name to \uc2 KFC\uc2  in 1991, in part to distance itself from the word ''fried,'' which had become associated with unhealthy eating. It currently has 30,000 restaurants worldwide.\par \par \uc2 A number of big companies have recently relocated to Texas. \uc2 Chevron\uc2 , the second-largest U.S. oil company, said in August that it would move its base to Texas from California, breaking up with a state that has pushed aggressively to address climate change.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , who is increasingly critical of what he sees as heavy-handed regulation in Delaware and California, has shifted many of his companies to Texas, including the carmaker \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , the rocket company \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and the social network X.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 , the owner of \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and WhatsApp, is also considering shifting its legal home from Delaware to another state, possibly Texas, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reported last month. And last year, a group of major financial firms funded a start-up stock exchange in Dallas to challenge the dominance of the New York Stock Exchange and Nasdaq in the listing and trading of companies and funds.\par \par \uc2 Danielle Kaye contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Danielle Kaye contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250220el2k0004t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
