\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Aviation; Southwest Plans to Cut 1,750 Corporate Positions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Niraj Chokshi \par \uc2 488 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The company said the cuts, the first round of broad layoffs in the airline's 53-year history, would affect mostly corporate employees.\par \par \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  on Monday announced plans to cut 1,750 jobs, the first broad layoffs in the airline's 53-year history.\par \par \uc2 The company said the cuts would mostly focus on corporate positions, accounting for about 15 percent of that work force. The layoffs will include 11 senior leaders with titles of vice president or higher, the airline said. Most of the cuts will be carried out by the end of June.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Southwest's chief executive, \uc2 Bob Jordan\uc2 , called the decision ''unprecedented.''\par \par \uc2 ''We are at a pivotal moment as we transform \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  into a leaner, faster and more agile organization,'' he said. ''I arrived at this decision thoughtfully and carefully, knowing how hard it will be to say goodbye to colleagues who have been a significant part of our Southwest culture and accomplishments.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jordan's own job was under threat last year after the hedge fund \uc2 Elliott Management\uc2  amassed an approximately 10 percent stake in the airline and began to push for widespread change, including Mr. Jordan's ouster. Elliott had accused Mr. Jordan and the airline's board of complacency and failing to control costs, eroding profit margins that were once the envy of the industry.\par \par \uc2 In response, Mr. Jordan laid out a three-year plan to make sweeping changes, including dropping the airline's seat-yourself policy in favor of assigned seating, adding seats with extra legroom and introducing red-eye flights -- the first of which began last week -- to make more use of its planes.\par \par \uc2 Southwest also agreed to add board members recommended by the investment firm, and Elliott ultimately dropped its demand for Mr. Jordan's departure.\par \par \uc2 The job cuts announced on Monday will save Southwest about $210 million this calendar year and $300 million next year, the airline said. But those figures do not include a one-time cost of $60 million to $80 million to pay out severance and other benefits to laid-off workers.\par \par \uc2 Southwest had an unrivaled 47-year streak of annual profits until 2020, when it lost money along with the rest of the industry during the Covid pandemic. It has reported profits each year since and remains the only one of the four largest U.S. airlines to have never filed for bankruptcy protection, though its costs have outpaced those of some of its peers.\par \par \uc2 Still, the airline, which offers only limited international flights, is a behemoth: Southwest carries more passengers and operates more flights in the United States than any other carrier. The airline is also beloved by fliers, who have routinely given its economy class the highest customer satisfaction scores of any carrier, according to \uc2 J.D. Power\uc2 , a market research firm.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250219el2j0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
