\par\par \uc2 NEW YORK TODAY\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Off Broadway, Labor Tension Heats Up\b0\par\par\uc2 By James Barron \par \uc2 1,662 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Stagehands and other backstage workers have gone on strike against a prominent theater, and two productions have been canceled.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. It's Thursday. Today we'll find out about the push to unionize backstage crews Off Broadway. We'll also get details on the Trump administration's seizure of $80 million that was supposed to cover some of the city's expenses for housing migrants.\par \par \uc2 For more than a month, striking stage crews have idled the nonprofit Atlantic Theater Company, where the musicals ''Spring Awakening'' and ''Kimberly Akimbo'' ran before they moved to Broadway and won Tony Awards. The strike is part of a unionization push that could change the economics of Off Broadway, which was hobbled by the coronavirus pandemic and has yet to recover. I asked Michael Paulson, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 's theater reporter, to explain where things stand.\par \par \uc2 You write that unionization will change the economics of theaters in New York. Will unionization drive nonprofit theater companies out of business? Can nonprofit theater companies afford to pay what unions demand?\par \par \uc2 Those are all questions that different people have different answers to. This unionization effort is taking place at for-profit and nonprofit entities, but nonprofits are having a particularly hard time right now. They're doing fewer shows. The number of shows eligible for the Lucille Lortel Awards this season is roughly half what it was five years ago. Costs have gone up since the pandemic and, if labor costs rise, that will be another financial challenge.\par \par \uc2 But the people who work backstage say the salaries they are being paid are not enough to live on. They say that just as theaters face financial challenges, so do workers, and the system needs to figure out how to pay living wages.\par \par \uc2 Who's looking to unionize?\par \par \uc2 It's people who work backstage, including the people we usually think of as stagehands -- people who move scenery around -- and also people who work in hair, makeup, audio, video, lighting and props.\par \par \uc2 Crews at five Off Broadway entities have voted to unionize over the last year.\par \par \uc2 Two of the five entities where crews voted to unionize are commercial musicals. The crew of ''Titan\u237\'00\'EDque,'' which spoofs the film ''Titanic,'' approved a contract last October, and the crew at ''Little Shop of Horrors,'' a revival of the sci-fi musical comedy, followed in January. The other three entities are nonprofits: the Public Theater, a large nonprofit; the Atlantic, which is a midsize operation; and the Vineyard, which is smaller.\par \par \uc2 What about the actors?\par \par \uc2 The crews are among the only workers at Off Broadway theaters who are not unionized -- the actors unionized decades ago -- and the crews are unionizing shop by shop.\par \par \uc2 ''Titan\u237\'00\'EDque'' and ''Little Shop'' began before the pandemic and are running indefinitely, so they're established operations. It would be difficult to organize a musical that has a five-week run -- by the time you could take the vote, everything would be over. But everyone involved believes this is the beginning of an effort to organize as many of the established Off Broadway entities as possible -- the institutional companies and the long-running shows.\par \par \uc2 What are the crews' particular concerns? Working conditions? Health insurance?\par \par \uc2 The concerns that I hear mostly are compensation, health care and retirement benefits. But there will be other issues on the table, including work rules, which concern overtime and staffing practices.\par \par \uc2 Off Broadway has historically been scrappier and more experimental than the commercial Broadway world. That often meant that Off Broadway employed people who were at the earliest stages of their careers, and often did not pay them particularly well, with the understanding that they were in a training ground. I think there was a time when people thought that because the work was noble and important they were willing to suffer for the work. One of the realities of the current climate is that fewer people now think that way. The prevailing idea is that work is work and should be compensated appropriately.\par \par \uc2 Off Broadway has matured over time. In its origins, the production values were low, the finances were anemic, and the sector identified as if everything was being made by groups of hungry young artists creating together in basements and other found spaces.\par \par \uc2 That is no longer the case for a significant portion of Off Broadway. That kind of work still happens. But we're also talking about mature institutions with multimillion-dollar budgets and development offices and sophisticated production values. The Off Broadway sector has changed over time, and these workers are asking for the way they are treated to change, too.\par \par \uc2 What about the Atlantic?\par \par \uc2 The Atlantic is an important theater that has birthed a lot of shows that have gone on to great success, but like many other nonprofits, it is facing financial challenges because of rising costs postpandemic. Negotiations there broke down in mid-January.\par \par \uc2 There were two plays in previews at that time. The productions have now been canceled. And it's not clear what's going to happen to the Atlantic's spring season.\par \par \uc2 For both sides, this is existential. The company says it's worried that labor costs will go so high it won't be able to continue to exist. And the workers worry that compensation is so low they can't continue to do theater as a career.\par \par \uc2 How much was the push to unionize a reaction to the pandemic? How does this fit with efforts to unionize at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 I think the labor movement emerged from the pandemic energized. We're seeing, all across the country in a variety of different industries, workers in companies that had not been unionized asking whether unionization might help them. People who work on Off Broadway crews know what's happening at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  or \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  -- and know unionized workers in other jobs in the theater world -- and think, Wow, would my work conditions be better if I did that? Some of them are answering yes.\par \par \uc2 Expect rain and clouds in the morning leading to sunny conditions in the afternoon, with a high in the mid-40s. In the evening, there will be clear skies and moderate winds with a low around 30 degrees.\par \par \uc2 Suspended for snow cleaning.\par \par \uc2 The latest New York news\par \par \uc2 Brooklyn's housing crisis forces change: A contentious plan to build two 10-story towers illustrates how a pressing shortage of affordable apartments is changing the politics of development.\par \par \uc2 Former aide accused of bribery: Linda Sun, a former aide to Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , faces new allegations related to charges that she used her position to benefit the Chinese government.\par \par \uc2 The Manhattan D.A. and the Adams case: With the Justice Department ordering federal prosecutors in Manhattan to drop corruption charges against Mayor Eric Adams, the legal spotlight could shift to the Manhattan district attorney, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Blocked fire hydrants: Two people died in a fire at two buildings in the Bronx, including one that housed a Buddhist temple -- the second fatal blaze in a week where firefighters had to work around illegally parked cars blocking hydrants.\par \par \uc2 The White House took back $80 million for migrant shelters\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration took back $80 million that the \uc2 Federal Emergency Management Agency\uc2  paid New York City last week. The money, seized from New York City accounts, was supposed to cover some of the city's expenses for housing migrants.\par \par \uc2 The office of the city comptroller, Brad Lander, noticed on Wednesday that the $80 million had disappeared from the bank accounts. The move by the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , of which FEMA is a part, was a significant escalation of President Trump's attempts to freeze or reverse funding that had previously been appropriated by Congress.\par \par \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , the homeland security secretary, asserted on X that the city was using FEMA funding to finance the use of the Roosevelt Hotel in Midtown Manhattan as a migrant shelter. She claimed that the hotel had served as a ''base of operations'' for the Venezuelan gang Tren de Aragua.\par \par \uc2 Liz Garcia, a spokeswoman for Mayor Eric Adams, said that City Hall had communicated with the White House and had requested an emergency meeting with FEMA to ''try and resolve the matter as quickly as possible.'' She added that City Hall was conducting an ''internal investigation into how this occurred,'' and that the city's Law Department was ''already exploring various litigation options.''\par \par \uc2 On Monday \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  said on X that his cost-cutting team had identified a $59 million payment from FEMA to pay for housing migrants at hotels in New York City. City officials said that the $59 million was part of the $80 million payment from FEMA last week.\par \par \uc2 The fallout from Musk's post led to the firing on Tuesday of four employees of FEMA, including its chief financial officer, who was accused of undermining the agency's leadership by disbursing the $80 million.\par \par \uc2 Sunday Phone Call, Nov. 24, 2024\par \par \uc2 Dear Diary:\par \par \uc2 An old voice in my head says it's Sunday\par \par \uc2 just loud enough so I pause.\par \par \uc2 I wrote about our Sunday chats\par \par \uc2 thirty years ago when you could kvell,\par \par \uc2 hearing your voice in the poem,\par \par \uc2 asking, ''Do you have things in the house?''\par \par \uc2 Things: groceries and treats.\par \par \uc2 Some grandmas need to know\par \par \uc2 what's in your icebox.\par \par \uc2 And I know your number SP7-4072,\par \par \uc2 the Spring Street ''exchange,''\par \par \uc2 a neighborly mystery.\par \par \uc2 It's Sunday afternoon,\par \par \uc2 so I thought I'd call.\par \par \uc2 -- Rachel Eisler\par \par \uc2 Illustrated by Agnes Lee. Send submissions here and read more Metropolitan Diary here .\par \par \uc2 Glad we could get together here. See you tomorrow. -- J.B.\par \par \uc2 P.S. Here's today's Mini Crossword and Spelling Bee . You can find all our puzzles here .\par \par \uc2 Natasha Cornelissen and Ed Shanahan contributed to New York Today. You can reach the team at nytoday@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Sign up here to get this newsletter in your inbox.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250217el2h00032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
