\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 The Federal Workers Trump Has Purged, So Far\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amy Schoenfeld Walker, Ashley Wu, Jon Huang and Elena Shao \par \uc2 1,061 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Trump has moved swiftly to wage his war on the federal bureaucracy, defying norms and legal limits. On Tuesday, he signed a new executive order to limit new hiring and commence what he called a ''critical transformation'' of government.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has specific targets: foreign aid workers, inspectors general, Jan. 6 investigators, and diversity, equity and inclusion workers. He has also given broad powers to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's government efficiency operation, which has inserted itself into more than a dozen agencies in search of spending and staffers to cut. Many of those agencies had been investigating Mr. Musk's companies.\par \par \uc2 Courts have temporarily halted some of the administration's efforts but many more layoffs across the government are expected in the weeks ahead. Here are the cuts and temporary removals that The Times has confirmed as of Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 'Wood Chipper' Victims\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump and Mr. Musk have made it clear they intend to shut down the nation's aid agency, \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  The administration began by putting nearly 60 senior staffers on leave and letting internal records be examined by what has been called the Department of Government Efficiency, an initiative led by Mr. Musk. ''We spent the weekend feeding \uc2 USAID\uc2  into the wood chipper,'' Mr. Musk posted on X on Feb. 3.\par \par \uc2 Days later, the Trump administration said it would drastically reduce the aid agency's work force, from more than 10,000 down to just a few hundred staffers. A judge temporarily halted the effort for more than 2,000 employees. Mr. Trump's 90-day freeze on foreign assistance has also put thousands of nonprofit and private sector jobs in limbo.\par \par \uc2 Employees of the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2 , a financial industry watchdog, may be next. Mr. Musk posted ''CFPB RIP'' on X on Feb. 7. The next day, the acting director of the bureau told the staff of more than 1,700 to effectively halt work.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has also proposed closing other agencies, including the Education Department and FEMA, where he fired the chief financial officer and three others on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 D.E.I. Workers\par \par \uc2 Only a few agencies have reported these figures, and this list is incomplete.\par \par \uc2 In late January, the Trump administration ordered agencies to put staff members with oversight of diversity, equity and inclusion efforts on leave, though some affected workers have said they had no connection to D.E.I. in their daily work. Within 24 hours of his inauguration, the Trump administration also fired the \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2  commandant for supposed mismanagement and an ''excessive focus'' on diversity within the service, according to a \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  statement.\par \par \uc2 The president also removed two Democratic commissioners and the general counsel from the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2 , an independent agency that fights workplace discrimination. The commission's new acting chair said her priorities would include ''rooting out unlawful D.E.I.-motivated race and sex discrimination.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump signed an executive order on his first day in office to rescind federal support for D.E.I. policies -- rolling back initiatives aimed at reversing discrimination and systemic inequities that have disproportionately affected Black people, women and underserved communities.\par \par \uc2 Trump Investigators\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department's campaign of retribution against officials who investigated Mr. Trump and his supporters has led to abrupt upheavals at the agency.\par \par \uc2 In late January, the acting attorney general fired more than a dozen prosecutors who worked on the two criminal investigations into Mr. Trump for the special counsel Jack Smith, saying they could not be trusted to ''faithfully implement'' the president's agenda.\par \par \uc2 Days later, the department fired more than a dozen prosecutors in the U.S. Attorney's Office in Washington who had been hired to investigate the Jan. 6 riot. The same day, the Justice Department's leadership ordered the Federal Bureau of Investigations to gather names of the thousands of agents involved in the Trump and Jan. 6 investigations.\par \par \uc2 At least nine high-ranking officials at the F.B.I. have also been removed since Mr. Trump's term began.\par \par \uc2 Government Watchdogs\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration fired at least 17 inspectors general in a late-night purge on Jan. 24.\par \par \uc2 The cuts defied a law that requires presidents to give Congress 30 days' notice and detailed reasons for the removal of the watchdogs, who work to uncover corruption and fraud across government agencies. In Mr. Trump's first term, inspectors general investigated at least eight of his cabinet members, according to \uc2 Campaign Legal Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the inspector general for \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  was fired, a day after he released a report warning that agency cuts risked the misuse and waste of hundreds of millions of taxpayer dollars.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump also fired the three Democrat-selected members of the Privacy and Civil Liberties Oversight Board, an independent agency that investigates national security activities that can intrude upon individual rights. Only one Republican member remains, and another seat is vacant, leaving the board unable to take official action.\par \par \uc2 On Feb. 10, Mr. Trump fired the director of the \uc2 Office of Government Ethics\uc2 , who was involved in vetting the new Trump administration appointees for potential conflicts of interest. Mr. Trump also removed the director of the \uc2 Office of Special Counsel\uc2 , a government watchdog agency, but a judge has temporarily blocked this firing.\par \par \uc2 Other Key Staffers\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has also moved to fire individual members of certain agencies, including the chairwoman of the \uc2 Federal Election Commission\uc2 , the nation's archivist, and the general counsel and a Democratic board member of the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 , which protects workers' rights.\par \par \uc2 The firing of the labor board's general counsel was widely expected in normal administration turnover. But the removal of the board member, who was meant to serve out a five-year term, is without modern precedent. The board now has just two members; under federal law, it needs at least three members to act.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's new staff has also carried out broad removals of employees within their agencies: The State Department fired about 60 contractors who work for its Bureau of Democracy, Human Rights and Labor. And Mr. Trump's national security adviser sent about 160 National Security Council workers home.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250213el2d0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
