\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Google\uc2  Abandons Employee Diversity Targets\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nico Grant \par \uc2 516 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Silicon Valley giant also said it was reviewing other diversity programs and initiatives and would update them if they seemed risky or ineffective.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  tried for years to diversify its work force, saying it would hire and promote more women and people from racial minorities.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the company abandoned employee diversity targets, saying that as a federal contractor, it needed to comply with President Trump's executive orders opposing diversity, equity and inclusion policies.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, \uc2 Google\uc2  announced a goal of increasing the number of people from underrepresented groups in its leadership team by 30 percent within five years.\par \par \uc2 ''In the future we will no longer have aspirational goals,'' \uc2 Fiona Cicconi\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 's chief people officer, said in Q&A that was emailed to employees and viewed by The New York Times.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Google\uc2  spokeswoman said that the company was ''committed to creating a workplace where all our employees can succeed and have equal opportunities'' and that it had been reviewing its programs over the last year. The email was reported earlier by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Like other tech giants, \uc2 Google\uc2  responded to a D.E.I. backlash bolstered by Mr. Trump's election victory. \uc2 Meta\uc2 , which owns \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , has eliminated many of its diversity teams, while \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has been reviewing its D.E.I. programs.\par \par \uc2 On Jan. 22, Mr. Trump signed an executive order instructing federal contractors to not engage in D.E.I., which he described as ''illegal discrimination.'' Through its cloud-computing arm, \uc2 Google\uc2  provides technology services to the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  has also started a D.E.I. review that could result in the company's cutting additional programs and initiatives, Ms. Cicconi said in the email.\par \par \uc2 The company will ''carefully'' evaluate programs, trainings and initiatives ''and will update them as needed,'' she said. The company will consider whether some of them ''raise risk'' or ''aren't as impactful as we'd hoped.''\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  reported last year that 5.7 percent of its work force was Black, up from 3.7 percent in 2020. And 7.5 percent of its employees were Hispanic or Latino, compared with 5.9 percent in 2020.\par \par \uc2 After the killing of George Floyd and ensuing protests in 2020, \uc2 Google\uc2  was one of many companies in the United States that pledged to hire and promote more Black and Latino workers. Part of \uc2 Google\uc2 's approach to improve representation was to expand its offices in cities like Atlanta and Washington.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Ms. Cicconi said that the company would continue to invest in places outside California and New York, and that the company would also hire in many countries globally. She added that the company would continue to have employee resource groups in which workers from similar backgrounds can convene.\par \par \uc2 ''We've always been committed to creating a workplace where we hire the best people wherever we operate, create an environment where everyone can thrive and treat everyone fairly,'' Ms. Cicconi said. ''That's exactly what you can expect to see going forward.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250207el2700035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
